@ 17.5 - Proto-Indo-European to Proto-Celtic
# citation: dhokarena56, from Matasović (2009), Etymological Dictionary of Proto-Celtic (ed. Lubotsky). “PIE Dialectal” Early Proto-Celtic Late Proto-Celtic
	— he he he > e a o
	— eh eh eh > e:[+long] a:[+long] o:[+long]
	—:[+laryngeal] > a / C_C // #_
	— SS > s:[+long]
	— ∅ > a / CS_:[+laryngeal]C
	—:[+laryngeal] > ∅ / V_C when pretonic
	—:[+laryngeal] > a / #S_C
	— Ḱ > [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]
	— ɡʷ > b
	— h > ∅ / C_
	— ∅ > i / C{l,r}S
	— e > a / _Sa (short a only),
	— ∅ > a / C_SC
	—:[+laryngeal] > ∅ / if not in a syllabic position
	— p…kʷ > kʷ…kʷ
	— e:[+long] > i:[+long]
	— o:[+long] > u:[+long] / in %#
	— V:[+long] > V:[-long] / _SC
	— CC > xC / if C was a plosive or s
	— p > b / :{ _r, _l }:
	— p > w / [+back]_N
	— p > f
	— o:[+long] ej > a:[+long] e:[+long]
	— e > o / _w
	— u > o / _wO